              "title": "Social Conformity",
              "paragraphs": [
                "People frequently adjust their opinions and behaviors to match those around them, even when doing so contradicts their own perceptions. This tendency toward conformity operates largely below conscious awareness and affects decisions ranging from consumer purchases to moral judgments. Understanding why individuals yield to group pressure reveals fundamental aspects of human social psychology.",
                "Classic experiments by Solomon Asch demonstrated the power of conformity through a simple perceptual task. Participants judged which of three lines matched a standard line in length, an easy determination when made alone. When confederates unanimously gave obviously wrong answers, however, roughly thirty-five percent of participants conformed to the incorrect group response at least once during the trials. Follow-up interviews revealed that some participants genuinely doubted their own perception, while others knowingly gave wrong answers to avoid standing out. {{A}}",
                "Cultural background shapes conformity rates in measurable ways. {{B}} Collectivist societies, which emphasize group harmony and interdependence, typically show higher conformity than individualist cultures that prize personal autonomy and self-expression. {{C}} Age also matters: adolescents conform to peers more readily than adults do, reflecting the developmental importance of social acceptance during that period. {{D}} These variations suggest that conformity serves adaptive functions whose expression depends heavily on social context."
              ],
