              "script": "So, let’s talk about something really fascinating—how the human brain evolved over millions of years. Understanding this history helps us appreciate why our brains work the way they do today.\nOur earliest ancestors, living several million years ago, had brains roughly the size of a chimpanzee’s—about 400 cubic centimeters. These early brains were sufficient for basic survival: finding food, avoiding predators, and simple social interactions.\nThen, something remarkable happened. Starting around two million years ago, brain size began increasing dramatically. Scientists believe several factors drove this change. First, our ancestors started eating more nutrient-rich foods, including meat, which provided the energy needed to support larger brains. Second, the development of tools required more complex thinking and planning.\nAs brains grew larger, new regions developed, particularly the prefrontal cortex—that’s the area right behind your forehead. This region handles abstract thinking, planning for the future, and controlling impulses. It’s one of the last parts of the brain to fully develop, not maturing until our mid-twenties.\nBy the time modern humans appeared around 300,000 years ago, brain size had nearly tripled compared to our earliest ancestors. However, it’s important to note that size isn’t everything. The organization of the brain and the connections between neurons matter just as much, perhaps even more, than overall volume."
