              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "So today, let’s discuss inflation—what it is and why it happens. This is something that affects everyone, so it’s important to understand the basics.\nInflation, simply put, is when the general price level of goods and services rises over time. When inflation occurs, each unit of currency buys fewer things than it did before. So if you could buy a sandwich for five dollars, that’s inflation at work.\nNow, what causes prices to rise? There are two main mechanisms. The first is called demand-pull inflation. This happens when there’s too much money chasing too few goods. If people have more money to spend but the supply of products stays the same, sellers can raise prices because buyers are willing to pay more.\nThe second type is cost-push inflation. This occurs of product increase. For example, if the prices of oil rises, transportation becomes more expensive. Companies then pass these higher costs on to consumers through higher prices.\nCentral banks try to manage inflation by controlling the money supply and interest rates. A little inflation—usually around two percent annually—is actually considered healthy for an economy. It encourages spending and investment. However, when inflation gets too high, it can seriously damage an economy because people’s savings lose value and planning for the future becomes difficult."
