              "scriptOrigin": "script-docx",
              "script": "Alright, today we’re going to talk about the relationship between sleep and learning. This is something that directly affects all of you as students. Research shows that sleep plays a crucial role in how well we remember and learn new information.\nSo what happens when we sleep? During sleep, especially during a stage called deep sleep, our brains are actually very active. They’re processing and organizing the information we encountered during the day. Think of it like a librarian sorting books. While you’re awake, information comes in randomly. During sleep, your brain files everything in the right place so you can find it later.\nStudies have shown that people who sleep well after learning something new remember it much better than people who stay awake. In one experiment, participants who slept after studying performed significantly better on tests than those who didn’t sleep. This effect is strongest when sleep happens soon after learning.\nNow, here’s what this means for students. Staying up all night to study, what we call cramming, is actually counterproductive. You might cover more material, but your brain won’t retain it properly without sleep. A better strategy is to study in shorter sessions and get adequate rest. Your brain needs that downtime to consolidate what you’ve learned."
